A ternary in a var initializer, and in a provider's value, compiles on Go.

Go has no conditional expression, so passTernary hoists a ternary into the
statement list before it -- and an initializer has none. The pass skipped
initializers on the reading that the optimizer had already folded any
ternary there, which is true of a constant condition and false of
`on ? "b" : "c"`: that reached every Go emitter as an ir.Ternary and
panicked it. A provider's value lands in the same position once passContext
seeds a hidden var from it.

Such a ternary is now a func literal called where it stands, whose body is
the `if` a hoist would have written. A nested ternary in either arm is
hoisted into that body like any other.

-- main.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
        fyne
        gtk4
    }
}

context #label("a")

component show ui.node {
    ui.text(value=label)
}

ui.window #home(title="t", href="/") {
    var on = false
    var n = 0
    var greeting = on ? "b" : (n > 1 ? "c" : "d")

    ui.vbox {
        ui.button(text="flip", @click { on = !on })
        ui.text(value=greeting)
        label(on ? "x" : "y") {
            show()
        }
    }
}
